<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</h1><p class="st-meta">12 CE – 428 CE (approx.) · capital Vagharshapat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Armenia under a branch of the Parthian Arsacid dynasty, which in the early 4th century made Christianity its state religion and later created the Armenian alphabet.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxiad-dynasty">Artaxiad</a> line ended, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> passed through years of rival kings installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>. The Arsacid branch took hold when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiridates-i-of-armenia">Tiridates I</a>, brother of the Parthian king, was crowned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> in Rome in 66 CE, a compromise that followed the Roman defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rhandeia">Rhandeia</a> and let both empires claim a stake.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a> over powerful hereditary noble houses such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamikonian-dynasty">Mamikonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratunis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiridates-iii-of-armenia">Tiridates III</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> around 301 through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral">Etchmiadzin</a> cathedral was founded. Around 405 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesrop-mashtots">Mesrop Mashtots</a> created the Armenian alphabet, launching a translation literature. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> was a battleground between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 387 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> partitioned <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a>, leaving most of it as a Sasanian client. In 428 Armenian nobles asked the Persian king to remove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-iv">Artaxias IV</a>, and the monarchy was abolished; the east became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/persarmenia">Persarmenia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)" width="320" height="312"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Tigranes_II_the_Great,_Antioch_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>12 CE – 428 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vagharshapat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tiridates I, Tiridates III, Artaxias IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrian-influenced paganism, then Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arsacid Armenia, Arshakuni Armenia, Greater Armenia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=220&amp;focus=kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid" aria-label="Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5759 795 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6079 1051L6079 